      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright (c) 2009 S. Karger AG, Basel.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "We undertook this study to assess the relationship between presentation pattern and mortality in patients with acute coronary syndrome (ACS) undergoing percutaneous coronary intervention."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "This registry included 10,455 patients with ACS, of whom 2,853 patients had ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction(STEMI), 3,060 patients had non-ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI) and 4,542 patients had unstable angina. The primary outcome was 1-year mortality."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "At 1 year there were 976 deaths, 390 (13.7%) among STEMI patients, 366 (12.0%) among NSTEMI patients and 220 (4.8%) among patients with unstable angina (OR = 1.17, 95% CI 1.01-1.35 for STEMI vs. NSTEMI; OR = 3.00, 95% CI 2.56-3.51 for STEMI vs. unstable angina, and OR = 2.58, 95% CI 2.20-3.04 for NSTEMI vs. unstable angina). In the Cox proportional hazards model ACS form was an independent correlate of 1-year mortality (HR = 0.90, 95% CI 0.73-1.13 for STEMI vs. NSTEMI; HR = 1.56, 95% CI 1.13-2.14 for STEMI vs. unstable angina; HR = 1.72, 95% CI 1.30-2.29 for NSTEMI vs. unstable angina)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "The presentation pattern affects 1-year mortality in patients with ACS, with unadjusted mortality being highest in patients with STEMI and lowest in patients with unstable angina."
          }
        ]
      },
